<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sidetic-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sidetic language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sidetic was an Anatolian language spoken at Side on the Pamphylian coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, related to Luwian and Lycian. It is known from a few inscriptions and coin legends of the 5th to 2nd centuries BCE, written in a distinctive alphabet of its own. Only partly deciphered, it shows that the Greek colonists of Side came to use the local language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidetic-language.jpg" alt="Sidetic language" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans van Deukeren · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Pamphylian_inscriptions.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Coins from Side bearing legends in an unknown script were first found in the 19th century. In 1914 an altar with a Greek and a Sidetic inscription came to light at Side, but the Sidetic text could not be read until a second Greek–Sidetic bilingual was found in 1949, which allowed Hellmut Theodor Bossert to identify 14 letters. In 1964 a large stone block near the east gate of Side yielded two longer texts, and in 1972 a text was found outside the city for the first time, at neighbouring Lyrbe-Seleukia. The longest inscription, a list of names of six complete lines also read as the Athenodoros memorial, was found in 1995.</p><p>The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, in his Anabasis Alexandri of the mid-2nd century CE, mentions a peculiar indigenous language at Side. Two words survive in Greek writers: zeigarê, cicada, recorded by the lexicographer Hesychius, and laerkinon, valerian, cited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>. Some puzzling signs in the third book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a>' Epidemics may be notes by the physician Mnemon of Side in Sidetic script.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sidetic was written from right to left in an alphabet of 31 letters in the inscriptions and four more found only on coins, a few of them clearly taken from Greek. The values of two thirds of the letters are now firmly established. The frequent vertical strokes are read by most scholars as a sibilant, which would fit an Anatolian genitive ending, and by others as word dividers.</p><p>By the time of the inscriptions the language was strongly influenced by Greek, with loanwords such as istratag, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a>, commander, and anathema-, votive offering. Few words go back to Luwian roots: maśara, for the gods, and perhaps malwadas, votive offering. No verb has been securely identified, though ozad, he offered, is a promising candidate with the common Anatolian past ending -d. As in neighbouring Pamphylian, names often lose their first vowel or an inner one, as in Poloniw for Apollonios and Artmon for Artemon. The Sidetic alphabet was added to the Unicode Standard in September 2025, in version 17.0.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sidetic language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to legend, they soon forgot Greek and spoke the local language, called <b>Sidetic</b>, written in a unique alphabet.</blockquote><a href="/state/side">Side</a></li></ul></section>
